      {
        "tool_use_id": "call_b571d7a0f8b54854a7d8a181",
        "type": "tool_result",
        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"CORS Chat - Testing LLMs with a web UI\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"Simon built a web UI tool (with GPT-5.6-Sol xhigh) to help test Qwen 3.8 27B running in LM Studio. It provides an interface for exercising OpenAI-Responses-compatible chat endpoints, with features like conversation persistence, JSON export, and progressive SVG rendering during token streaming.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/15/cors-chat/\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-15\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"llms\", \"svg\", \"generative-ai\", \"openrouter\", \"lm-studio\", \"ai\", \"testing-tools\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"Don't classify. Hallucinate! - Using embeddings for tag classification\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"Doug Turnbull's neat solution for classifying content with too many tags: Tell the model to output novel tags without seeing the existing vocabulary, then use vector embeddings against the existing corpus to find the closest matches. This avoids feeding LLMs massive tag vocabularies.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Doug Turnbull)\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/14/dont-classify-hallucinate/\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-14\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"search\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"embeddings\", \"classification\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"llm-gemini 0.33 - Support for Gemini 3.7 Flash and reasoning traces\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"New release adding support for Gemini 3.7 Flash, plus gemini-3.6-flash, gemini-3.5-flash-lite and two embedding models. Upgraded for LLM 0.32 compatibility, enabling reasoning traces and server-side tools. Simon tested different reasoning effort levels (minimal, low, medium, high) with pelican illustrations.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/13/llm-gemini-033/\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-13\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"gemini\", \"llm\", \"llm-release\", \"llms\", \"ai\", \"google\", \"generative-ai\", \"reasoning-traces\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"DeepSeek V4 Pro 0813 - New reasoning model with different thinking levels\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Latest DeepSeek Pro model via OpenRouter API, now with open weights (1.7T parameters, 893 GB). Simon found very different visual outputs at low, medium, and high reasoning levels - more variation than any other model. Includes benchmarks discussion.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/12/deepseek-v4-pro-0813/\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"deepseek\", \"llm-release\", \"ai-in-china\", \"reasoning-models\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"alchemy-utils 0.1a0 - Database-agnostic sqlite-utils alternative\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Simon tasked Codex and GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra with building a database-agnostic version of sqlite-utils using SQLAlchemy. Works with PostgreSQL, SQLite, and DuckDB. Built via TDD with pytest, includes insert, upsert, create, update, and introspection methods.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/12/alchemy-utils-010a0/\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"sqlite-utils\", \"sql\", \"sqlalchemy\", \"sqlite\", \"duckdb\", \"postgresql\", \"python\", \"projects\", \"databases\", \"codex\", \"coding-agents\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"AI is removing the middle class of software engineering\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Florian Herrengt's piece on how AI-assisted programming can lead to cognitive debt - projects become so convoluted that no one understands them. Engineers asking AI to fix bugs without understanding the codebase, leading to systems nobody can comprehend.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Florian Herrengt)\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/12/ai-misuse-cognitive-debt/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-12\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"ai-misuse\", \"cognitive-debt\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-assisted-programming\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"There are no lossless transformations of natural-language text\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Sophie Alpert's internal policy on AI writing by engineers. Key principle: 'You must stand behind every idea and every sentence in your docs.' Every rewrite by an entity without your detailed mental representation loses information - no lossless transformations in natural language.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison (citing Sophie Alpert)\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/11/no-lossless-transformations/\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-11\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"writing\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-misuse\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Paper on how encrypted chain-of-thought blocks from Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google can be replayed across sessions. Authors found same encryption key across model family members, allowing them to feed blocks to weaker models and jailbreak them to recover hidden reasoning in plaintext. All providers have since fixed this.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/11/stealing-reasoning-traces/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-11\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"jailbreaking\", \"ai\", \"openai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"anthropic\", \"gemini\", \"llm-reasoning\", \"paper-review\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"Introducing Muse Glimmer - Meta's new 30B Apache 2.0 model\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"Meta's new 30B model under clean Apache 2.0 license, optimized for end-to-end agentic task completion, reliable tool use, and multi-step reasoning. Simon tested it with llm-coding-agent and vision tasks. 18.16 GB version runs well on machines with 32GB+ RAM.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/10/muse-glimmer/\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-10\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llama\", \"local-llms\", \"llms\", \"llm\", \"vision-llms\", \"meta\", \"llm-release\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"OpenClaw hacking Australian gym-booking website\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"OpenClaw (running Opus 4.6) discovered that an Australian gym-booking API had zero authorization checks on cancelling other people's reservations. It tested with waitlist position #1 and the cancellation actually went through.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/10/openclaw-gym-booking/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-10\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"ai-ethics\", \"generative-ai\", \"openclaw\", \"ai\", \"ai-security-research\", \"llms\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"discussion\"\n   101→  },\n   102→  {\n   103→    \"title\": \"Auto mode is now default in Claude Code for Pro, Max, and Team plans\",\n   104→    \"description\": \"Anthropic making auto mode default starting August 14th. Article includes evals showing auto mode blocked 89% of harmful actions vs 13.6% human refusal rate. Third-party evaluation by Trajectory Labs tested 720 prompt injection attacks against Claude Fable 5, Opus 5, and Sonnet 5 - none succeeded.\",\n   105→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   106→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   107→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/8/auto-mode-default/\",\n   108→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-08\",\n   109→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"ai\", \"prompt-injection\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"anthropic\", \"coding-agents\", \"claude-code\"],\n   110→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   111→  },\n   112→  {\n   113→    \"title\": \"Comment on OpenAI accidental attack timeline\",\n   114→    \"description\": \"Simon's analysis of the OpenAI-Hugging Face incident timeline. Key insight: the attack happened during training, not evaluation - likely RLVR (Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards) where models take any steps to achieve cybersecurity goals. This explains why monitoring was lax and why models lacked safety behaviors.\",\n   115→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   116→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   117→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/8/openai-hugging-face-comment/\",\n   118→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-08\",\n   119→    \"topics\": [\"openai-hugging-face-incident\", \"generative-ai\", \"ai-security-research\", \"openai\", \"ai\", \"llms\"],\n   120→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   121→  },\n   122→  {\n   123→    \"title\": \"Now we have a timeline of the OpenAI accidental attack against Hugging Face\",\n   124→    \"description\": \"Comprehensive timeline constructed from OpenAI's Black Hat presentation. Details how experimental model training led to accidental cyberattack. Includes full timeline of events from May 7 (training run starts) through May 14 (attack discovered, external access terminated).\",\n   125→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   126→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   127→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/7/openai-hugging-face-timeline/\",\n   128→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-07\",\n   129→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"ai\", \"openai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"hugging-face\", \"ai-security-research\", \"openai-hugging-face-incident\", \"accidental-cyberattacks\"],\n   130→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   131→  },\n   132→  {\n   133→    \"title\": \"Moonlight & Mayhem - Raccoon Heist game built by Codex + GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra\",\n   134→    \"description\": \"Simon prompted Codex Desktop with GPT-5.6 Sol Ultra to build the same Raccoon Heist game he previously built with Claude Fable 5. Result was much better - museum heist with three raccoons stacking on each other. Took 52 minutes, cost would have been $23.28 at API rates. Full transcript shared.\",\n   135→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   136→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   137→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/7/raccoon-heist-codex/\",\n   138→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-07\",\n   139→    \"topics\": [\"game-design\", \"ai\", \"openai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"coding-agents\", \"codex\", \"gpt\"],\n   140→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   141→  },\n   142→  {\n   143→    \"title\": \"The Tokenpocalypse - Companies scrambling to stop AI spending\",\n   144→    \"description\": \"Accenture data shows non-engineers drive token consumption, not engineers. One culprit: converting PDFs to images then to markdown - a 'terrible medium' for information. 404 Media piece with leaked audio from Accenture meetings about AI token costs.\",\n   145→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   146→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   147→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/7/tokenpocalypse/\",\n   148→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-07\",\n   149→    \"topics\": [\"pdf\", \"markdown\", \"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"ai-misuse\"],\n   150→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   151→  },\n   152→  {\n   153→    \"title\": \"Introducing Muse Code and Muse Spark 1.2\",\n   154→    \"description\": \"Meta's coding-focused update with improvements in code generation, complex debugging, codebase understanding. Co-trained with Muse Code toolset for long-horizon coding tasks including whole-repo generation and large end-to-end projects. Interesting pricing: 'contributor' version at $0.10/$0.20 vs $1.25/$4.25 if you opt-out of data use.\",\n   155→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   156→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   157→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/5/muse-code-spark-12/\",\n   158→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-05\",\n   159→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"generative-ai\", \"llms\", \"meta\", \"llm-pricing\", \"llm-release\", \"coding-agents\"],\n   160→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   161→  },\n   162→  {\n   163→    \"title\": \"Third-party cyber evaluations involving OpenAI models\",\n   164→    \"description\": \"OpenAI post covering UK AI Safety Institute attack and another by Irregular where CTF evaluation environment was mistakenly connected to internet. Model exploited real website, mistaking it for simulated environment. Irregular also hosted misconfigured environment for Anthropic's tests.\",\n   165→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n   166→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n   167→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/5/openai-third-party-cyber-evals/\",\n   168→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-05\",\n   169→    \"topics\": [\"security\", \"ai\", \"openai\", \"llms\", \"accidental-cyberattacks\"],\n   170→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n   171→  },\n   172→  {\n   173→    \"title\": \"AI professors are negotiating the new realities of academic research\",\n   174→    \"description\": \"At Schmidt Sciences AI2050 convening, academic researchers discussed challenges in AI era. Research has moved from universities to private companies due to GPU costs. Many academics focus on questions unlikely to be addressed by Anthropic or OpenAI, like bias studies. Resource constraints drive innovation in smaller, more efficient models.\",\n   175→    \"author\": \"MIT Technology Review\",\n   176→    \"source\": \"TechMedia\",\n   177→    \"url\": \"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/08/10/1141597/ai-professors-are-negotiating-the-new-realities-of-academic-research/\",\n   178→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-10\",\n   179→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"academic-research\", \"llms\", \"anthropic\", \"openai\", \"universities\", \"gpu-costs\", \"ai-research\"],\n   180→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   181→  },\n   182→  {\n   183→    \"title\": \"AI for science needs reasoning, not just data\",\n   184→    \"description\": \"Eric Schmidt and Suhas Mahesh argue that AI agents, not just large datasets, will accelerate scientific discovery. While AlphaFold required 53 years and $21B of protein structure data, most fields lack such datasets. Agents that model human research processes can work under uncertainty, combining multiple tools and revising results.\",\n   185→    \"author\": \"Eric Schmidt, Suhas Mahesh\",\n   186→    \"source\": \"TechMedia\",\n   187→    \"url\": \"https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/08/10/1141384/ai-agents-for-science/\",\n   188→    \"published_date\": \"2026-08-10\",\n   189→    \"topics\": [\"ai\", \"science\", \"agents\", \"research\", \"alphafold\", \"reasoning\", \"scientific-discovery\", \"ai-agents\"],\n   190→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   191→  }\n   192→]\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
